  "summary": "I wrote the solar-system explorer in `src/main.ts`. It passes `typecheck`, but I never ran it. I had no shell or browser, so `vite build` and the rendering are untested.\n\n- **Scene:**\n  - A glowing star with a corona, a point light and bloom, on a dark background with 900 tiny stars on a far shell.\n  - Eight planets, Mercury to Neptune, each with its own orbital distance, size, colour and speed. Inner planets move faster.\n  - Planet surfaces use coloured spots and bands made from primitives, so spin is visible. I didn't use textures or shaders because I couldn't check that they render.\n  - One thin circular orbit line per planet, built as a flat mesh strip.\n  - Earth and Jupiter each have a moon. Saturn has a tilted two-band ring.\n- **Focus:**\n  - Clicking a planet (picked by projecting each planet to the screen with a 12 px minimum tolerance) or calling `window.__focusPlanet(name)` starts a 1.5 s eased camera move, then the camera keeps following the planet.\n  - A panel at the bottom-left shows the name, orbital period in Earth days and distance in million km.\n  - Clicking empty space, pressing Escape or calling `window.__unfocus()` eases back to the overview and hides the panel.\n- **Overview controls:** Dragging orbits the camera, with the pitch limited. The scroll wheel zooms between distances of 28 and 180.\n- **Pause button:** The Pause/Play button is at the bottom-right. It freezes orbits, spin and the moons.\n- **State and ready flag:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only getter returning `{ready, focused, paused, planets, cameraDistanceToFocus}`. `ready` and `window.__EVAL_READY__` are set after the first two animation frames following `app.ready()`.\n\nI made three guesses about the library that only a browser run will confirm:\n- **Camera control:** I assumed a custom camera rig passed to `app.camera.use` works. The docs say this is on by default.\n- **Light strength:** The sun's point light uses `decay: 1`, `intensity: 16`, with extra ambient and emissive fi",
